**user**:
> Here is a chapter from a report I am writing:
> ===
> Chapter 2: Macroeconomic and Fiscal Risks
> Macroeconomic risks refer to the potential threats to a country’s economic stability and growth. Key indicators such as Gross Domestic Product (GDP) per capita growth rate and GDP-equivalent welfare loss serve as primary metrics for assessing economic performance and societal well-being. A decline in GDP per capita signals stagnation, while welfare loss quantifies the broader impacts of economic stressors, highlighting the relationship between economic health and quality of life.
> 
> In addition to these primary indicators, secondary measures like total factor productivity (Letta & Tol 2019) and labour productivity (Rode et al. 2024) shed light on the productive foundation of the economy. High productivity levels indicate a robust economy, while declines suggest underlying problems. Other important factors include capital depreciation, which affects investment capacity, as well as interest rates, inflation, and unemployment, all of which can significantly influence economic growth and public finances.
> 
> Fiscal risks encompass the potential challenges that can undermine a government’s financial stability and its ability to fund public services. Understanding these risks is essential for maintaining fiscal health and ensuring sustainable economic growth.
> 
> Primary indicators, such as public expenditures, revenues,  borrowing costs, and debt, are crucial for assessing a government’s financial dynamics. High borrowing costs, influenced by sovereign credit ratings, can strain public finances and limit a government’s ability to finance essential services or infrastructure projects (Klusak et al. 2023). Furthermore, the growth of sovereign debt can signal potential future challenges related to repayment and fiscal discipline.
> 
> It is also important to consider assets at risk from physical impacts, particularly in the context of climate change. Such risks can lead to unexpected expenditures or losses, further complicating a government’s fiscal landscape, and these already exist as contingent liabilities.
> 
> Macroeconomic outcomes such as GDP loss and the other items above represent only part of the damaging consequences of climate change (Stern et al. 2022). Climate change is expected to undermine health and human capital, reduce ecosystems stability and natural productivity, and disrupt communities and cultures. GDP and macroeconomic indicators do not capture many of these effects. Alternatives, such as inclusive wealth accounting and welfare calculations, provide a more comprehensive picture but no one measure is sufficient for basing decisions on.
> 
> At the same time, GDP loss is a crucially important indicator for key processes. First, as a measure of consumption and exports, GDP is a strong predictor of government revenues. While the elasticity of tax revenue varies depending on the structure of the economy in a country and its tax policies, declines in GDP usually result in corresponding declines in tax revenue. Second, while consumption is a poor predictor of well-being at higher income levels, it is an important input to it at lower levels of income. For poorer countries, GDP correlates with life expectancy and many other indicators of well-being.
> Impacts on Gross Domestic Product
> Using a subset of the studies concerned with top-down macroeconomic impacts, we look at impacts of climate change on GDP at the national level. These results are based on a meta-analysis of 85 econometric models from 15 macroeconomic studies of the impacts of temperature on GDP growth (models listed in Appendix figure 1). The meta-analysis method combines Monte Carlo draws from each study, according to how well it predicts future GDP growth estimates beyond the period on which each model was calibrated (see appendix A.2). We also include the effects of tipping points on global temperatures (Dietz et al. 2021), market losses from sea-level rise and storm surge (Depsky et al. 2023), and an estimate of the effects of trade spillovers (Rising 2023) . Dietz et al. (2021) represents the most complete meta-synthesis of tipping points from economic modeling studies, while Depsky et al. (2023) is a significant update to the forward-looking coastal adaptation model CIAM.
> As a meta-analysis of macro-econometric results, several important features are missing, generally due to the limitations of current econometric methods. Importantly, these models do not account for non-market welfare losses, such as impacts on health and the biosphere, which are expected to be large; this is missing because it is outside the intended scope of these analyses. Total economic damages (including market and non-market losses) are expected to be considerably higher than these estimated market losses. In addition, our synthesis of the results misses (1) impacts that are not strongly driven by temperature shocks, such as slow onset effects, water supply and demand impacts, and other extreme events, such as surface and river floods, droughts, and wildfire; (2) regional-scale tipping points such as AMOC and sea-level rise tipping points; and (3) future adaptation and the results of broader changes in the economy. These aspects are each included in some form in some macroeconometric studies, but we do not project these aspects of the models in our reanalysis.
> On average, climate change lowers GDP by 8% in 2050. Using this evidence base, we find that under the high emissions scenario, the average person lives in a country that is projected to be 8% [3 - 15%] poorer in 2050, in terms of GDP per capita, than it would have been without climate change. The greatest losses are across low and lower-middle income countries and at low latitudes. In particular, sub-Saharan Africa and Southeast Asia face the largest losses, with some countries expected to lose 20% of their GDP due to climate change by 2050.
> Some individual study estimates are significantly higher such as those used by the Network for Greening the Financial System (NGFS), which relies on Kotz et al. (2024a), and Bilal & Känzig (2025), primarily due to longer persistence of impacts and accounting for the role of global-scale temperatures. Our estimates are a synthesis of 85 estimates and hence represent an average, which is likely to change over time as the representation of extreme events and other factors improve. In particular, the lack of extreme events suggests that this may be a significant underestimate.
> Percentage losses in GDP in 2050 under High Emissions
> 
> Figure 1. Colours and labels show the loss in GDP due to warming from 1960 to 2050, under an high-emissions baseline scenario, as a percent of each country’s counterfactual GDP without climate change. The estimate is based on econometric studies and accounts only for temperature shocks. Countries for which the interquartile range of uncertainty crosses 0 are shown with a diagonal pattern and faded labels. Not all countries are labelled, to reduce visual clutter.
> 
>  
> Low and lower-middle income countries are hit the hardest. There is a strong income gradient in these losses, with climate change reinforcing existing inequalities. Least developed countries are projected to be already 10% [5 – 15%] poorer due to climate change today, 14% in 2050, and 20% by 2075. Because of the spatial inequality of these impacts, global GDP is projected to be only 5% lower in 2050, nearly half of the average person impact..
> Percent losses in GDP by Economic Group
> 
> Figure 2. Loss in GDP due to warming since 1960, under a high-emissions baseline scenario, by economic group and year. The estimate is based on econometric studies and accounts only for temperature shocks. Error bars show the interquartile range.
> 
>  
> High income countries in the Middle East and North Africa and the East Asia & Pacific regions face large losses as well, and are expected to be 15% and 8% poorer, respectively, in 2050.
> Percent losses in GDP by UNFCCC Party Group
> 
> Figure 3. Loss in GDP due to warming since 1960, under an SSP3-7.0 scenario, by UNFCCC party group and year. The estimate is based on econometric studies and accounts only for temperature shocks. Error bars show the interquartile range.
> 
> 
> These impacts have consequences also for the number of families in poverty. An inter-model comparison found that India could have between 2 and 60 million more people under a reference scenario.
> Capital and welfare losses
> Previous work using the same econometric evidence base has found that produced capital (World Bank 2024e) is already lower across much of the world today because of climate change (Rising 2023). At the same time, reduced economic activity– under the current economic system– can actually benefit ecosystems, reducing the rate of their exploitation and increasing renewable natural capital. This can partly offset the direct losses to these ecosystems driven by climate change. Between produced and renewable natural capital, all economic groups have a lower capital base because of climate change than they would otherwise have had.
> 
> Percent losses in GDP by Economic Group
> 
> Figure 4. Loss in GDP due to warming between 1960 and 2023, under an SSP3-7.0 scenario, by economic group. The estimate is based on econometric studies and accounts only for temperature shocks. Error bars show the interquartile range.
> 
> In contrast to labour productivity, which is mostly impacted by rising temperatures, capital loss is driven primarily by acute shocks from extreme weather events and sea-level rise. Physical capital depreciation is larger in countries exposed to cyclones, particularly where there is a higher share of population inhabiting low-lying coastal areas (Bakkensen et al., 2020). Floods, storms, and cyclones are expected to decrease global net asset value by an average of 4.4% by 2050 under the current policies scenario (Marcelo et al., 2023). In China, storm-surge losses have a significant economic impact on coastal areas (Yin et al., 2024). In the USA, damages to coastal areas represent the highest economic losses from climate change (EPA, 2017). In Côte d'Ivoire under the SSP3-7.0 scenario, capital damaged by storm surges due to sea-level rise is equivalent to 1.2% of GDP in 2050 (World Bank, 2023a).
> Capital loss reduces current private income flows that generate welfare. With capital loss, private consumption is reduced because there is a greater reallocation of GDP components, such as household consumption, towards investment. In disaster-prone countries, consumption is expected to be reduced by 5.3% compared to non-disaster-prone countries (Cantelmo et al., 2023). According to the CDP Climate Change 2022 survey of firms, climate change is estimated to reduce asset value by 5% (Task Force on Climate-Related Financial Disclosures, 2023). Earnings could be reduced by 5-25% in a scenario exceeding 3°C warming, due to asset damage and business interruption from weather shocks (World Economic Forum, 2024).
> As mentioned above, total welfare losses are expected to be much higher than GDP losses. We use a meta-analysis of 105 damage estimates from 38 studies (Howard & Sterner 2025) to evaluate welfare impacts along our standard high-emissions scenario and for the year 2050 (see appendix A.3). These damages are reported as GDP-equivalent losses—that is, the amount of consumption loss that produces the same welfare loss as produced by the full range of welfare impacts.
> According to this synthesis, total damages in 2050 reach a level equivalent to 14% [8 – 19%] of global GDP. Almost 50% of damages are attributable to catastrophic impacts. The definition of catastrophic damages varies across the underlying studies in the meta-analysis, but generally represents tipping points or other uncertain thresholds of economic risk. A third of damages is attributable to the effects of climate change on economic growth rates, rather than just considering the immediate impacts in a given year.
> Welfare losses in GDP-equivalent terms in 2050
> 
> XX Waterfall plot of the damages from Howard & Sterner (2025). Each column shows a component of the total and its corresponding uncertainty (shown as the interquartile range).
> 
> 
> Recent estimates of the welfare loss and damage due to climate change vary significantly, reflecting a range of assumptions and methodologies. Markandya & González-Eguino (2018) use an integrated assessment modeling approach to estimate that non-Annex I countries experienced losses ranging from $179 to $671 billion per year as of 2020. In contrast, Songwe et al. (2022) anticipate losses based on engineering estimates to reach $150 to $300 billion annually by 2030. Examining extreme weather events, Newman & Noy (2023) attribute $53 billion per year to market damages and an additional $90 billion per year to losses valued through the Value of Statistical Life (VSL) methodology. Accounting for GDP and capital losses, Rising, 2023 uses econometric studies to estimate $4 trillion per year in damages by 2023. Together, these studies underscore the substantial and varied economic impacts of climate change, highlighting the urgent need for tailored mitigation and adaptation strategies.
> Labour productivity impacts
> We estimate labour productivity loss in the future by synthesising 8 studies that report national, regional, or global labour productivity loss in percent terms, under a current policies scenario. Estimates from these studies are pooled and the distribution reported, as described in appendix A.4. Studies range in their inclusion of adaptation: Some macro-modeling studies (e.g., World Bank 2022) include autonomous adaptation (actions taken by the agents individually) but exclude adaptation policies in the results presented here (adaptation costs and benefits are studied in the adaptation results section), while such others rely on experimental studies that include no adaptation effects. Econometric studies include autonomous adaptation, but in some cases also represent reductions in vulnerability as regions become richer or experience more heat (e.g., Rode et al. 2022).
> Labour productivity is expected to be reduced in much of the world (see figure Figure 5), with parts of Africa and Asia experiencing over 5% reductions by 2050. There is considerable uncertainty, with an analysis in Switzerland suggesting that there is a 1-in-20 chance that labour productivity losses are four times their expected value. Simultaneously, growth rates in labour productivity are expected to be impacted by climate change. In low and lower-middle income countries, the rate of warming between now and 2050 is expected to decrease labour productivity growth by 0.05 percentage points per year, which would result in an additional 1% loss in labour productivity (Letta & Tol 2019).
> 
> Labour Productivity and Total Factor Productivity Impacts from Climate Change in 2050.
> 
> Figure 5.. Expected impact on labour productivity and total factor productivity growth, due to climate change, from a synthesis of studies. Studies used different scenarios, but only scenarios that can be considered middle-of-the-road, current policies, or high-emissions scenarios are included. Interquartile ranges (error bars) cover both within-study and across-study uncertainty.
> 
> 
> Heat stress-induced labor productivity decline has greater economic impacts when economies rely heavily on weather-exposed activities and face rising temperatures. In the regions most affected by warming, the costs of increased worker disutility (their loss of the personal value of their time due to heat) are projected to be equivalent to 12% of GDP. In cold regions and with low-risk workers exposed to weather changes, the effects on labour productivity are expected to be positive, such as in Northern Europe (Rode et al., 2022). A study suggests that in Côte d'Ivoire, the most significant impact of climate change is projected to stem from the impact of heat stress on labour productivity (World Bank, 2023a). In the Sahel region, modelling indicates that the biggest channel of GDP loss is also due to the decline in labour productivity with increased temperatures (World Bank, 2022). Similar findings are reported in studies carried out in the Democratic Republic of Congo (World Bank, 2023b), Malawi (World Bank, 2024b), the Republic of Congo (World Bank, 2023c), the Dominican Republic (World Bank, 2023d) and Moldova (World Bank, 2024c). 
>  
> In high-income economies, rising temperatures may also generate significant economic losses driven by the impact of heat stress on labour productivity, although their proportional impact on GDP is lower (Rode et al., 2022). A study indicates that in the US, productivity losses from heat stress represent the most substantial temperature-related economic impact (EPA, 2017). In Australia, labour productivity loss is projected due to increasing temperatures is a significant mechanism that will impact the economy, especially outdoor productive activities. According to available modelling, the sector most affected by heat stress on labour productivity in Australia is manufacturing, as its output will be reduced by 0.32% in a below 3°C scenario (Australian Government, 2023). 
> 
> Other macroeconomic outcomes
> Total factor productivity (TFP) growth is expected to be lowered by climate change. This is based on combining the results of three econometric studies, reporting the pooled synthesis as described in appendix A.4. These include autonomous adaptation. The largest expected reductions in growth are in Africa, at 0.05 percentage points lower per year. There is considerable uncertainty around these numbers, however, with a 1-in-4 chance that TFP is 0.3 percentage points lower per year across much of the world, which would result in a 7% loss in TFP by 2050.
> 
> These indicators of productivity have consequences for capital investment. Greater economic activity drives greater investment into produced capital, and climate impacts can undermine this investment process. Reduced investment also results in a lower productive capital base, driving a feedback loop that can result in an even greater gap in economic potential.
> 
> Effects of droughts and higher temperatures are more significant for the economy when the agricultural sector constitutes a relevant share of the country's economic activities. Countries with a larger agricultural sector are more vulnerable to harsh droughts than countries that don't rely on these weather-exposed activities and are exposed to higher temperatures (Akyapi et al., 2025). The CCDRs from the World Bank highlight that the higher vulnerability of countries depends on their reliance on agriculture for employment and income in dry/hot scenarios (World Bank, 2024d).  
>  
> In Kenya, the main driver of GDP loss in a dry/hot scenario is crop output reduction and erosion caused by higher temperatures (World Bank, 2023e). In Morocco, water yield declines will cause a reduction of 2.8-7.1% of GDP due to reduced agricultural productivity caused by water shortage (Pérez-Cutillas, 2024). In Greece, direct economic losses related to agricultural output decline are between 437 million and 1 billion euros per year due to extreme temperature events (Georgopoulou et al., 2024). 
> 
> Acute shocks in temperatures have a short-term effect on inflation, but not in the long-term. Higher temperatures increase inflation persistently for up to 12 months in both higher and lower income countries (Qi et al., 2025). An increase of 1% in temperature leads to an increase in the inflation rate of 0.0189%, according to econometric analysis based on historical data. However, a single shock does not have persistence over more than one year (Lo et al., 2023). A study by the Network for Greening the Financial System (2025) suggests that in the long-term there can even be deflation due to a recovery in production. 
> 
> Nevertheless, constant weather shocks could cause persistent upwards pressures on inflation every year. In this sense, global inflation per year will be expected to increase by 1.79 percentage points under a RCP8.5 scenario by 2035 (Kotz et al., 2024b).
> 
> 
> 
> Projected increases in Inflation Rates in 2050
> 
> Figure 6. Increases in inflation rates, driven by physical and transition risks. Estimates combine Kotz et al. (2024b) and Boirard et al. (2022) as described in appendix A.4.
> 
>  
> Evidence suggests that multiple mechanisms drive this price increase, with food prices being the primary factor pushing this macroeconomic indicator upward. Agricultural prices increased in 121 countries when there was an increase in temperature (Yusifzada, 2024). In fact, food inflation has been found to be higher than headline inflation when there are climatic shocks. Another mechanism is related to interest rates. With extreme weather events, there is economic contraction and therefore central banks reduce interest rates, which in turn increases inflation (Qi et al., 2025). A shortage in energy supply due to climatic shocks could also raise prices (Li et al., 2023). The impact of weather shocks on supply chains and therefore on inflation has been found to be relevant in some countries, but not all (Cevik et al., 2024). 
> 
> Global warming significantly impacts unemployment rates. As temperatures rise, inflation and disruptions in economic activity and agricultural output can lead to declining asset values and business activities, which can increase unemployment due to deflationary pressures. Using econometric methods, Liu & Lin (2023) find that countries located between 20 and 40 degrees latitude experience higher unemployment linked to global warming, while regions above 40 degrees may see a reduction in unemployment. Furthermore, the effects of climate change vary across economic contexts, with middle-income countries bearing a greater burden and male unemployment being more affected than female unemployment. Long-term implications such as decreased labor productivity and increased labor costs in certain sectors further complicate this dynamic. Consequently, it is essential for policymakers to develop adaptive economic policies that address these climate-induced changes in unemployment dynamics to ensure resilient financial and labor systems.
> 
> 
> Change in unemployment rate in 2050
> 
> Figure 7. Changes in unemployment rate driven by warming under an SSP3-7.0 scenario with tipping points, projected using estimates from Liu & Lin 2023.
> 
> 
> Climate impacts can drive increases in inequality. Since climate change disproportionately affects vulnerable populations, disrupting livelihoods. Emmerling et al. (2024) extend an ensemble of eight large-scale integrated assessment models with income groups to study this effect. Under a scenario without climate policy, the Gini coefficient, as an index of inequality, is projected to rise by 2050. As climate damages amplify existing disparities, they can contribute to greater economic instability and social unrest. Conversely, implementing policies aligned with the Paris Agreement, and redistributing the carbon revenues from those policies on an equal per-capita basis (EPC) more than counteracts these inequality increases. This approach transforms potential risks into benefits by using redistribution as an adaptation mechanism.
> Change in the Gini index in 2050, with and without redistributive policies
> Figure 8. Estimates for 2050 under the No Climate Policy and Paris with equal per capita redistribution scheme, from a model intercomparison by Emmerling et al. (2024).
> 
> Sovereign credit ratings
> Sovereign creditworthiness is increasingly shaped by the impacts of climate change. A sovereign’s credit rating influences its cost of borrowing, with direct implications for both public and private financing. Evidence shows that physical climate risks are already translating into sovereign downgrades and higher borrowing costs, particularly for emerging and developing economies, while the benefits of adaptation remain under-recognized (Volz et al., 2020; Zenios, 2022).
> 
> ​​Empirical research demonstrates that countries exposed to climate shocks face higher risk premia and increased default probabilities. For example, the 2022 floods in Pakistan affected 33 million people, caused USD 30 billion in damages, and led Fitch to downgrade the country’s credit rating, citing liquidity pressures exacerbated by the disaster (Fitch, 2022). Forward-looking models suggest that by 2030, nearly 60 countries could face average sovereign downgrades of 0.7 notches, rising to over 2 notches by 2100 (Klusak et al., 2023).
> 
> Recent downgrade data illustrates this trajectory. We apply an existing analysis to evaluate these impacts and find that all regions of the world are at risk of downgrades (Klusak et al. 2023). In contrast to our macroeconomic results, we also find that the greatest impacts of climate change on sovereign credit ratings fall on upper middle income and high-income countries, across all regions. 
> 
> Klusak et al. explains that the largest credit rating downgrades in high-income countries are due to the nature of sovereign credit ratings. High-rated sovereigns have further to fall because small increases in perceived default probability can lead to significant downgrades, unlike for lower-rated countries that are already viewed as high-risk. Although poorer countries are more vulnerable to climate impacts, their credit ratings may not reflect this vulnerability as drastically because they already face considerable political and economic challenges, which are factored into their lower ratings. The risk to credit worthiness for poorer countries is already present, with climate-vulnerable countries facing a measurable risk premium in their debt markets, which reflects both their heightened exposure to extreme events and their limited fiscal capacity to respond (Kling et al., 2018; Cevik & Jalles, 2021). Furthermore, the climate-economic models used in the study predict economic losses across all development levels, so the relative change in credit ratings appears more pronounced for those initially rated higher. This pattern reflects the structural design of credit assessments and market expectations rather than the direct economic vulnerability to climate change.
> 
> Sovereign credit downgrading across regions and income groups in 2050
> 
> Figure 9. Impact of climate change on sovereign credit ratings, evaluated under a high-emissions scenario by Klusak et al. (2023), and adjusted to represent 2050. Number labels report the number of countries available in each region-income group from Klusak et al.
> 
> 
> Despite these risks, adaptation efforts are rarely reflected in credit assessments. Large-scale investments in resilient infrastructure, early warning systems, and disaster risk finance can materially reduce fiscal vulnerability. However, credit rating agencies typically acknowledge such measures only when they yield immediate fiscal benefits (Andersen et al., 2025). This results in systematic undervaluation of adaptation, even though empirical work shows that adaptive capacity is a significant determinant of sovereign credit outcomes (De Moor et al., 2018; Cevik & Jalles, 2022). The downgrades illustrated in Figures 1 and 2 highlight what is at stake: without adaptation, countries in climate-vulnerable regions face mounting fiscal stress and escalating borrowing costs.
> 
> The mispricing of climate risk and adaptation creates a vicious cycle. Vulnerable economies are penalized with higher borrowing costs, which constrain their ability to finance adaptation, leaving them more exposed to the next shock. As Figures 1 and 2 demonstrate, this cycle disproportionately affects low- and middle-income countries. Breaking it requires policy and market reforms that recognize adaptation as a credit-enhancing factor. Priority measures include improved disclosure and integration of climate risks into debt sustainability analyses, the development of innovative financial instruments such as resilience bonds and contingent credit lines, and stronger support from multilateral development banks (ADB, 2019; EBRD, 2019).
> 
> Sovereign downgrades linked to climate impacts are no longer hypothetical—they are observable realities. Unless adaptation is systematically integrated into sovereign credit assessments, many vulnerable economies risk being trapped in a downward spiral of downgrades and rising debt costs. Embedding adaptation into financial analysis is therefore not only an environmental imperative but also a fiscal one (Klusak et al., 2023; Boston Consulting Group & GRP, 2022).
> Other fiscal impacts
> Government expenditure is expected to increase and government revenue is expected to decrease with climatic shocks. Nevertheless, changes in public debt are uncertain as climate change may downgrade countries' credit ratings. 
> 
> The effect of climate change on public expenditures shows a wide variation. While the Australian Government (2023) estimates an increase in public expenditures of 33% per degree warming for Australia, Leppänen et al. (2015) estimate a decrease of 1.5 to 2.6% per degree of warming for Russia.
>  
> At a global level, harsh droughts lead to a significant increase of 0.11 percentage points in the government expenditure-to-GDP ratio, and an increase in the occurrence of extreme hot days reduces the government revenue-to-GDP ratio by 0.14 percentage points (Akaypi et al., 2025). In Australia, the increased frequency and severity of natural disasters are expected to increase expenditure by 3 to 3.6 times over the following 40 years (Australian Government, 2023). In the US, the public expenditure increase caused by extreme hot days raises the social cost of carbon by 23-33% (Barrage, 2024). 
> 
> Nevertheless, evidence of the effect of climatic shocks on public debt is not conclusive. Two econometric studies based on historical data find that debt does not increase significantly after climatic shocks. According to a study from Akaypi et al. (2025), extreme temperatures or droughts do not have a significant impact on public debt at a global level. The authors find this result hard to interpret and do not provide any explanation for this finding.  A study in the US found that public debt was reduced 10 years following hurricane exposure. The main reason is that interest rates increased by 1% after hurricane exposure, due to the fact that these natural disasters increased the default risk by 0.1 percentage points. As a result, public debt did not increase, but government expenditure was reduced (Jerch et al., 2023).These findings suggest that governments might address fiscal deficits by reducing expenditure rather than relying solely on increased borrowing.
> 
> Other studies have used modeling approaches to understand debt impacts. Gagliardi et al. (2022) perform a stylised analysis of the fiscal impact of natural disasters, finding increases between 0.7 pp and 4.5 pp across EU countries under a 1.5 °C scenario and from 0.9 to 5.2 pp under a 2 °C scenario. Lamperti et al. (2019) use an agent-based model to evaluate the effects of capital and labour damages and find that the Debt-to-GDP ratio increases by up to 94% on average when climate change driven impacts on both are considered.
> 
> Interest rates are expected to increase annual interest payments on sovereign debt by US$45-67 billion under the RCP2.6 scenario and US$135-203 billion under the RCP8.5 scenario. 
>  
> The metric of fiscal (primary) balance seems ripe for analysis: climate change is likely to both reduce revenues and increase expenditures for governments, and the effect on their combination may be more tractable than either individually. However, evidence of fiscal balance is very sparse. Within our review, two papers provided this outcome: a stylised analysis of fiscal balance for EU countries due to natural disasters (Gagliardi et al.) and an analysis of optimal fiscal rules for Pacific Islands (Nakatani 2021).
> 
> Few studies consider the role of adaptation to alleviate fiscal risks. In Box 1, we showcase Integrated Water Resources Management (IWRM) as a key adaptation practice, because it provides substantial fiscal benefits by significantly reducing flood-related economic losses, safeguarding public infrastructure and revenues, and attracting private investment.
> 
> Box 1. Fiscal Benefits of Investing in Integrated Water Management to Reduce Flood Risk: Nigeria Case Study
> 
> Floods are Nigeria’s costliest and most frequent natural hazard. The 2022 floods alone caused an estimated US$6.68 billion in direct damages and up to US$9.1 billion in broader economic losses, straining federal and state budgets through emergency relief, rehabilitation of transport and energy assets, and expanded social protection needs (World Bank, 2023). Integrated water resources management (IWRM)—which coordinates land, water, and risk across basins and sectors, blending gray and nature-based infrastructure with data, regulation, and finance—offers a fiscally efficient path to reduce these losses. Global and regional evidence indicates benefit-cost ratios around 4:1 for resilient infrastructure and early-warning-enabled preparedness, with strong co-benefits for jobs, health, and productivity (Hallegatte et al., 2019). Applying IWRM in Nigeria’s priority basins, particularly the Niger–Benue, can meaningfully lower disaster spending, protect revenues, and crowd in private investment.
> 1.   Floods as a Structural Fiscal Risk
> Nigeria’s exposure to flood risk is rising due to rapid urbanization, watershed degradation, silted channels, and climate-intensified extreme rainfall. In 2018, coastal degradation, dominated by flooding and erosion, in just three states—Lagos, Delta, and Cross River—cost an estimated US$9.7 billion, equivalent to approximately 2.4% of national GDP, underscoring large, recurring welfare and fiscal impacts from flood-related service disruptions and health burdens (World Bank, 2020). The 2022 floods affected 64% of households nationally, with especially severe impacts on non-farm enterprises, 91% of which reported adverse effects (UNDP & NBS, 2023). These damages drove tax base erosion and higher emergency outlays. Federal estimates put economy-wide losses near US$9.1 billion, while rapid assessments reported US$6.68 billion in direct damages to housing, transport, agriculture, and public assets—costs that risk ultimately cascading to public budgets.
> 2.   Integrated Water Resources Management in Practice
> Integrated water resources management (IWRM) is a basin-scale approach that aligns water allocation, land use, flood protection, water quality, drought management, and ecosystem services under one governance and investment plan. It is grounded in the Dublin-Rio principles and embedded in Nigeria’s water governance through the Nigeria Integrated Water Resources Management Commission (NIWRMC) and River Basin Development Authorities—though implementation remains uneven (NIWRMC, 2021). In practice, a Nigerian IWRM package for flood risk reduction typically combines upstream and peri-urban nature-based solutions such as watershed reforestation and floodplain restoration, targeted gray works like dredging and urban drainage upgrades, real-time hydromet networks and flood early warning systems, as well as policy reforms including zoning, building control, and risk-layered financing (WRI, 2022).
> 3.   The Fiscal Case for IWRM
> The fiscal rationale for IWRM rests on several pillars. First, avoided damages to public infrastructure and services are significant. Evidence from the World Bank’s Lifelines study shows that every dollar invested in resilient infrastructure yields about four dollars in benefits, mainly by reducing repair costs and downtime (Hallegatte et al., 2019). IWRM also lowers emergency and social protection outlays. Early-warning-enabled preparedness reduces casualties and asset losses, with high benefit-cost ratios documented in low- and middle-income countries (UNDRR, 2019). By keeping firms operating and supply chains intact, IWRM also protects Nigeria’s tax base and economic output. For instance, in 2018 alone, coastal flooding and erosion eroded an estimated 2.4% of GDP in just three states (World Bank, 2020). Furthermore, IWRM interventions such as wetland restoration improve water quality and reduce the burden of water-borne diseases, lowering public health expenditure (World Bank, 2020). Finally, embedding resilience into infrastructure projects reduces risk perceptions and attracts private investment; while resilience features typically add 1–3% to upfront costs, they yield returns of around 4:1 and improve credit terms (World Bank, 2019).
> 4.   Illustrative Fiscal Benefits
> A hypothetical US$500 million IWRM flood-resilience program in the Niger–Benue basin, combining upstream nature-based solutions, urban drainage upgrades, and forecasting systems, could generate benefits worth around US$2 billion based on average BCRs. If early warning systems were to reduce just 10% of the US$6.68 billion in damages recorded in the 2022 floods, this would translate into approximately US$668 million in avoided losses in a single severe season (World Bank, 2023). In Lagos, Delta, and Cross River states, reducing coastal flood and erosion costs by 15–20% could protect between US$1.5 and 1.9 billion annually (World Bank, 2020). These back-of-the-envelope estimates suggest that IWRM investments can pay back within a single political cycle.
> 5.   Real-World IWRM Experiences in Nigeria
> Nigeria’s experience with IWRM illustrates both successes and persistent challenges. The Ibadan Urban Flood Management Project (IUFMP), launched after the devastating 2011 floods, represents a hybrid model that combined basin-scale planning, infrastructure investments, and an early warning system. By 2024, the project had rehabilitated 37 hydraulic structures, completed dam safety works, dredged 431 kilometers of rivers and drains, and installed early warning systems benefiting over 2.1 million residents. These investments, funded by a US$200 million World Bank credit, demonstrated how integrated planning reduces emergency repair costs, though sustainability hinges on formal adoption of masterplans and adequate operations and maintenance budgets (World Bank, 2024a).
> In northern Nigeria, the Komadugu–Yobe Basin Trust Fund was established by six riparian states and the federal government with AfDB support to coordinate dam operations at Tiga and Challawa Gorge. The goal was to restore downstream flows to the Hadejia–Nguru wetlands while reducing destructive floods. Benefits included improved fisheries, farming opportunities, and reduced conflicts between irrigation and downstream users. However, institutional complexity and the lack of enforceable operating rules have limited its full effectiveness (AfDB, 2015).
> The Nigeria Erosion and Watershed Management Project (NEWMAP), implemented across more than 20 states, rehabilitated degraded watersheds using bioengineering and drainage improvements. NEWMAP generated a net present value of US$317 million with a benefit-cost ratio of 1.4 and an internal rate of return of 15%. These results confirm that upstream rehabilitation reduces sedimentation, prevents infrastructure damage, and is fiscally cheaper than repeated dredging or road repairs (World Bank, 2022).
> By contrast, the Oyan Dam along the Ogun River illustrates the risks of incomplete integration. Seasonal releases regularly inundate downstream communities in Lagos and Ogun States. While authorities issue early warnings and relocate households, limited transparency on reservoir operations, weak enforcement of zoning laws, and solid waste clogging drains mean that flood losses persist year after year. This example demonstrates that early warning systems, though valuable, cannot deliver fiscal savings unless paired with transparent dam management and urban planning reforms (NEMA, 2024).
> 
> Research gaps
> Only 3% of sources reviewed take into account tipping points. Mid- and late-century impacts are likely underestimated, as a result. Generally, effects that emerge from future thresholds are not represented in these results. Both statistical and macromodeling approaches assume that the basic structure of the economy, stability of the climate, and relationship between the economy and the climate are relatively unchanged in the future. However, the “Reasons for Concern” in recent IPCC reports has highlighted the near-term risks that some of these unknown thresholds pose.
> 
> The role of extreme events and precipitation in general is understudied. For example, only impacts driven by temperature change and sea-level rise (coastal inundation and storm surge) are included in the GDP results above. While there has been some work to incorporate hazards other than temperature into econometric analysis of GDP, the contributions of these other factors is found to be much smaller than the temperature effects (Waidelich et al. 2024). The minor role of these other factors is likely a limitation in the ways that extreme events have been represented, and more work is needed to improve these representations.
> 
> As shown in Figure 10, there is a strong bias in the data for large countries, like the United States and China. Macroeconomic and fiscal risks across much of the lower income world remain poorly studied.
