> 
> 
> Studies on unemployment unrelated to energy transition mitigation scenarios and opportunities are limited. Therefore, it is important to examine how high temperatures, weather shocks, and sea level rise impact unemployment at the national level.
> 
> The review identified only two studies that analyzed the impacts of climate change on total factor productivity. This variable is crucial for explaining long-term economic growth.
> 
> Studies related to capital loss are typically static in nature and show the accumulation of capital loss over time. Given that capital loss represents a reduction in a country's wealth, this usually has implications for consumption, productivity, and therefore welfare over time. Consequently, there are opportunities for new research to focus on the long-term welfare impacts of capital loss. Additionally, studies quantifying natural and human capital loss due to climate change are also limited.
> 
> The interaction between the downgrading of sovereign credit ratings, increased public expenditure, and decreased public revenue caused by climate change should be studied in greater detail. There is insufficient evidence of how countries will address fiscal deficits, for example, which budget categories could be affected by reduced government revenues.
