> - **Macroeconomic and fiscal risks from climate change are profound, often outstripping GDP-focused assessments by affecting productivity, capital, and labor across sectors and geographies.**
> 
>   - Climate change is projected to reduce global GDP by 8% by 2050, disproportionately impacting low and lower-middle-income countries.
> 
>   - Labour productivity, heavily influenced by temperature increases, is expected to drop significantly, especially in regions reliant on weather-exposed labor.
> 
>   - Total factor productivity will likely decline in warmer climates, further exacerbating economic vulnerabilities.
> 
> - **Sovereign credit ratings are increasingly at risk due to climate change impacts, affecting borrowing costs and fiscal stability.**
> 
>   - High-income countries may face larger relative downgrades due to higher baseline ratings, though poorer countries are intrinsically more vulnerable.
> 
> - **Rising fiscal demands from climate change include increased government expenditures and decreased revenues, yet adaptation measures are inadequately credited within sovereign assessments.**
> 
>   - Strategic adaptation, such as Integrated Water Resources Management, offers significant fiscal benefits by minimizing climate-induced damages and protecting economic stability.
> 
> - **Current models inadequately capture non-market welfare losses and underrepresent the impacts of climate tipping points, limiting their insight into future economic challenges.**
> 
>   - The insufficiency of data, particularly for low-income countries and emerging economies, underscores the need for expanded, diverse macroeconomic analyses.
> 
